.TH "ELEMENT.POINTERUP_EVENT" "3JS" "July 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.pointerup_event \- Element: pointerup event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]pointerup\f[R] event is fired when a pointer is no longer
active.
Remember that it is possible to get a \f[CR]pointercancel\f[R] event
instead.
.PP
This behavior is different from \f[CR]mouseup\f[R] events.
When using a physical mouse, \f[CR]mouseup\f[R] events fire whenever any
button on a mouse is released.
\f[CR]pointerup\f[R] events fire only upon the last button release;
previous button releases, while other buttons are held down, don\(cqt
fire \f[CR]pointerup\f[R] events.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in methods like \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R], or set
an event handler property.
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqpointerup\(dq, (event) => { })

onpointerup = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A \f[CR]PointerEvent\f[R].
Inherits from \f[CR]Event\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
Using \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] para = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

para.addEventListener(\(dqpointerup\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqPointer up\(dq);
});
.EE
.PP
Using the \f[CR]onpointerup\f[R] event handler property:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] para = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

para.onpointerup = (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqPointer up\(dq);
};
.EE
